    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1100">

                <pb facs="1390" n="1380" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg1380"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.9331/10–247: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-10-02T18:00:00+09:00">October 2, 1947—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 2—6:27 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2016. When Embassy representatives October 2 informed Governor Central Bank
                    substance your telegram 1101, September 3, 4 p.m., he replied that he and K. P.
                        Chen<anchor n="30" xml:id="d1100-f1"/> had proposed that UTC activities
                    should consist of following in future: (1) liquidation paper commitments; (2)
                    purchases on Chinese Government account from private commercial channels in
                    United States; (3) promotion of Chinese exports without, however, any
                    participation in their commercial disposal.</p>
                <pb facs="1391" n="1381" xml:id="pg1381"/>
                <p>In Embassy’s opinion this proposal constitutes basis for discussions here and in
                    United States. For Department’s information, K. P. Chen stated to Embassy
                    official in Shanghai last week-end that CSC<anchor n="31" xml:id="d1100-f2"/>
                    staff being drastically curtailed.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 2016; repeated Shanghai 817.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1100-f1" n="30" xml:id="d1100-f1-content">
                <p>President of the Universal Trading Corporation.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1100-f2" n="31" xml:id="d1100-f2-content">
                <p>Chinese Supply Commission, official Chinese agency in Washington handling
                    procurement of lend-lease shipments. It was dissolved on August 1, 1947, except
                    for completion of “old business”, and its functions were transferred to the
                    Universal Trading Corporation.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
